rustgis-grass-plugin (0.7.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The panel's catalogue gains a Temporal mode. The mode tree now lists All
    modules, Imagery and Temporal with their counts, and the temporal mode
    shows only the 74 t.* modules.
  * A new Temporal ribbon group, between Vector and Modules, holds the panel
    and a Temporal Modules button that opens that mode. This is a catalogue,
    not a runner: a temporal module reads a space-time dataset rather than one
    raster, so every t.* module keeps its reason in the tree instead of
    offering a run that would fail.
  * The manifest now carries 38 commands, 64 tools, 7 ribbon groups and 23
    panel items, against 1,176 readable modules on this machine. The manifest's
    version is 0.7.0.

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Thu, 05 Nov 2026 10:00:00 +0100

rustgis-grass-plugin (0.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The panel's module catalogue has a mode tree: All modules or Imagery. The
    Imagery mode keeps the three branches but shows only the 176 i.* modules,
    labels the all branch accordingly, filters Recently run too, and says in
    the status line that imagery modules only are shown.
  * A new Imagery ribbon group holds the panel, an Imagery Modules button and
    menus for the seven i.* modules Run Module can drive from one raster:
    i.edge and i.zc under Enhance, i.destripe and i.sar.speckle under
    Preprocess, i.water and i.emissivity under Detect, and i.rotate under
    Geometry. An All Modules button in the Modules group restores the whole
    catalogue.
  * The Recently run branch is always present, labelled Recently run (0) until
    something has run, so the panel always has its three branches.
  * The manifest now carries 37 commands, 64 tools, 6 ribbon groups and 23
    panel items, against 1,175 readable modules on this machine. The manifest's
    version is 0.6.0.

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Wed, 04 Nov 2026 10:00:00 +0100

rustgis-grass-plugin (0.5.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The panel's catalogue now says what can actually be run: a module is
    offered to Run Module only when it reads one raster, writes one, needs no
    second required raster, and leaves every other required parameter to a
    default. On this machine that is 88 of 1,174 modules, counted when the
    manifest is generated rather than guessed. Modules that need several
    bands, an imagery group, coordinates, a config file, a display, a required
    flag, or another parameter are marked with the reason instead of being
    offered and failing after the wait.
  * The module tree has three branches: Runs from here, All modules (with the
    reason for each module that cannot be driven) and Recently run, the last
    twelve modules kept beside the results. Labels carry GRASS's summary and
    keywords so the tree's search finds a module by what it does as well as by
    name, and a leading triangle marks the ones Run Module can drive.
  * Choosing a module fills a Chosen module text under the tree with what it
    reads and writes, so a module that cannot run explains itself before the
    button is pressed. Run Module names the input and output the module
    actually requires (r.watershed's elevation, not its first optional input).
  * The modules that got a command of their own are now only those that can
    run that way: 18 of the curated 27 remain, in the same five menus.
  * The ribbon has a Modules group for the panel and Run Module, and Database
    no longer carries the panel.
  * The manifest's version is 0.5.0.

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Tue, 03 Nov 2026 10:00:00 +0100

rustgis-grass-plugin (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Tool parameters that take a file are offered instead of being left out:
    the rules of v.segment and v.lrs.segment, v.extract's category list,
    v.net's node file and v.rectify's control points. RustGIS hands the plugin
    the file the user picks, which is written into the run's own folder. A
    file a module writes but declares as an input (v.rectify's rmsfile) is
    still left out and reported, because a file picker cannot name a file that
    does not exist yet.
  * Rows a module writes without geometry come back as a standalone table
    rather than an undrawable layer, and are counted in the run's messages.
  * The common raster modules have a command of their own on the ribbon, in
    menus for terrain, hydrology, filters, surfaces and the sun. Every other
    module is still reached from the panel's tree.
  * Before a raster leaves, raster.info says what it is: a layer with no
    coordinate system is refused rather than worked on in cells, the source's
    size, cell size, coordinate system and value type are reported, and the
    panel's new band slider says which band of a multi-band raster GRASS sees,
    narrowed to the bands that raster has.
  * A run on a raster RustGIS holds only as a downsampled overview says so:
    the result is coarser than the file on disk.
  * The package carries a skill for RustGIS's AI Assistant, so it knows which
    requests GRASS is needed for at all, how the tools and raster commands are
    addressed, and that the computational region decides the rest.
  * The manifest states the RustGIS it needs (0.5.0, for panel trees and
    compact ribbon menus), its author, homepage and licence, and a French
    translation of the plugin's own interface text.

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 07:30:00 +0100

rustgis-grass-plugin (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A ribbon tab of its own, with groups for the database, the region, raster
    work and the vector tools, the last as menus by GRASS keyword.
  * Every installed module is offered. The forms of 64 vector modules are
    generated as geoprocessing tools, which is every tool a manifest holds,
    and all 1,172 modules the installation could describe are in a searchable
    tree in the panel. Run Module runs the chosen one on the chosen raster
    layer with GRASS's own defaults; the 357 that read a raster and write one
    are marked in the tree.
  * The module list comes from the installation itself, addons included, and
    the generator says how many modules it read, how many became tools and
    which ones it had to leave out.
  * Parameters RustGIS would refuse are left out and reported: a name with a
    digit in it (v.gsflow.segments's "width1") and a name the module declares
    twice (the same module does, four times over).

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 15:05:00 +0100

rustgis-grass-plugin (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The computational region comes from RustGIS's Analysis Environment: its
    extent becomes the region's bounds, clipped to the raster and aligned to
    its grid, and its cell size the resolution, converted to the raster's own
    units. The panel can set the extent to the map view or clear it, and
    carries a cell size of its own that wins over both.
  * r.slope.aspect is given -a whenever the region was narrowed. Without it the
    module puts the region back on the elevation map's own grid, and the cell
    size asked for is ignored with nothing said.
  * The panel reaches the GRASS database GRASS itself uses, found from GISDBASE
    or its settings file: projects and mapsets are listed from the file system,
    a mapset's maps when it is opened, and a chosen map is exported and added
    to the project. The export runs in a throwaway mapset, so the user's own
    mapsets are never locked. A button also puts the database in the browser's
    Folders, which is as near the Add Data menu as a plugin can get.
  * A GRASS warning wrapped over several lines is kept whole. It was cut at the
    first line, which left sentences such as "East-west resolution does not
    equal north-south resolution. The" in the run's messages.

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 14:20:00 +0100

rustgis-grass-plugin (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First package. Five GRASS vector modules as RustGIS geoprocessing tools,
    with every form read from the installed GRASS rather than kept by hand.
  * Three raster modules as commands with a panel: slope, aspect and viewshed.
    A plugin tool can take no raster and make no host request, so these export
    the chosen layer, run the module and add the GeoTIFF back, inside the two
    minutes RustGIS allows a command.
  * Each run reports the projection it worked in, the computational region it
    set and every distinct GRASS warning with a count. The region is never
    left at its default: a fresh project's one-cell region silently drops
    features.
  * rustgis-grass-setup generates the manifest from the GRASS on this machine
    and registers the plugin for the user who runs it.

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 13:55:00 +0100
